WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.382 --> 00:00:06.009
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:08.530
Where are you from?

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:11.209
Chicago.

00:00:11.209 --> 00:00:16.556
No, where are you really from?

00:00:16.556 --> 00:00:19.548
The South side side of Chicago?

00:00:19.548 --> 00:00:22.405
No, what are you?

00:00:28.785 --> 00:00:34.409
For years, I couldn't explain why
incidents like this made me feel so

00:00:34.409 --> 00:00:35.850
uncomfortable.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:40.300
You speak such good English,
you must be good at math.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.783
Your people's food is so spicy.

00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:50.881
Innocuous on the surface,
but when repeated 10,

00:00:50.881 --> 00:00:56.709
20, 50 times,
I would start to get annoyed.

00:00:56.709 --> 00:00:59.996
But these were just curious people
with good intentions, right?

00:00:59.996 --> 00:01:05.038
What right did I have to be annoyed?

00:01:05.038 --> 00:01:09.501
It wasn't until I was in
college that I realized what

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:13.970
I was experiencing were
racial microaggressions.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:19.075
Psychologist Derald Wing Sue
defines microaggressions as brief,

00:01:19.075 --> 00:01:23.196
everyday exchanges that send
denigrating messages to

00:01:23.196 --> 00:01:27.418
certain individuals based
on their group membership.

00:01:27.418 --> 00:01:31.426
While they can be the product
of environmental or

00:01:31.426 --> 00:01:36.717
behavioral slights,
most of the time they are unintentional.

00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:39.364
And they happen all the time.

00:01:39.364 --> 00:01:45.125
In fact, in a recent survey that
I sent out to my GSB classmates,

00:01:45.125 --> 00:01:52.616
77% of respondents identified as having
been the victim of a microaggression.

00:01:52.616 --> 00:01:54.226
So why do we care?

00:01:54.226 --> 00:01:57.042
Well, take this example.

00:01:57.042 --> 00:02:02.197
When a young woman repeatedly hears,
you have a great shot at that

00:02:02.197 --> 00:02:07.274
promotion because they need
a female on their leadership team.

00:02:07.274 --> 00:02:12.269
She starts to feel like she doesn't
deserve to be there based on

00:02:12.269 --> 00:02:14.070
her own merits alone.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.700
She starts to feel like
she doesn't belong.

00:02:18.726 --> 00:02:22.301
Think that was too extreme of an example?

00:02:22.301 --> 00:02:23.640
Take my opening question.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.184
No, where are you really from,

00:02:27.184 --> 00:02:32.444
implies that Chicago
cannot be a valid answer.

00:02:32.444 --> 00:02:34.474
I cannot be American.

00:02:34.474 --> 00:02:35.668
I must be a foreigner.

00:02:35.668 --> 00:02:43.065
I cannot claim to be from the place
that I'm from because of how I look.

00:02:43.065 --> 00:02:46.691
And I perpetually don't belong.

00:02:46.691 --> 00:02:50.704
Now, the purpose of my talk today
is not to play the victim or

00:02:50.704 --> 00:02:56.021
to even fantasize about a perfect world
where there are no microaggressions.

00:02:56.021 --> 00:03:02.248
That would be unrealistic, because of all
of the unique backgrounds, experiences,

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:08.068
and implicit biases that everybody on
this Earth carries with us, me included.

00:03:08.068 --> 00:03:15.653
Instead, I want to recognize
that these tiny snubs exist.

00:03:15.653 --> 00:03:20.907
All of us commit them, and
that's going to happen.

00:03:20.907 --> 00:03:24.679
While our intent is not
necessarily malicious,

00:03:24.679 --> 00:03:29.926
different comments land with
different people in different ways.

00:03:29.926 --> 00:03:35.830
And I'm here, firstly,
to bring awareness to that fact of life.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:40.961
And more importantly, to tell you
what I've learned we can do if and

00:03:40.961 --> 00:03:45.474
when we find ourselves on either
end of a microaggression.

00:03:49.036 --> 00:03:50.872
A couple of years ago,

00:03:50.872 --> 00:03:56.976
I had a boss casually throw me the advice
to act more like the men in the room.

00:03:59.256 --> 00:04:05.672
I was stunned, I had no words
to express how I was feeling.

00:04:05.672 --> 00:04:12.610
So I just said okay, and
walked back to my desk.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:19.873
Six months passed, and I heard those
words haunting me every single day.

00:04:19.873 --> 00:04:22.696
You should act more like a man.

00:04:22.696 --> 00:04:23.929
Being a woman is not good enough.

00:04:26.825 --> 00:04:32.601
My sense of self worth, my confidence,
and my productivity took a hit,

00:04:32.601 --> 00:04:38.201
as I desperately tried to figure out
how to make sense of that comment.

00:04:38.201 --> 00:04:39.000
How could we have avoided this situation?

00:04:43.345 --> 00:04:44.933
Let's turn to the receiving end.

00:04:44.933 --> 00:04:49.718
There are three things that you can do
when you find yourself the recipient of

00:04:49.718 --> 00:04:51.079
a microaggression.

00:04:51.079 --> 00:04:55.362
First and most importantly,
prioritize yourself.

00:04:55.362 --> 00:05:00.969
There are situations where it
doesn't make sense to respond.

00:05:00.969 --> 00:05:05.814
If I had gotten this comment from
a stranger I'd never see again, or

00:05:05.814 --> 00:05:10.574
perhaps if my safety could have
been compromised if I did respond,

00:05:10.574 --> 00:05:14.570
I would give myself the permission
to not say anything.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:19.834
And I know that it's unfair that
the emotional burden of whether or

00:05:19.834 --> 00:05:22.939
not to respond falls on the recipient.

00:05:22.939 --> 00:05:27.565
But the more comfortable we get
with drawing boundaries and

00:05:27.565 --> 00:05:34.057
predicting the implications to our lives
if we do not respond, the easier it gets.

00:05:34.057 --> 00:05:40.607
Second, if you're in a place to,
ask clarifying questions.

00:05:40.607 --> 00:05:47.674
I could have simply asked my boss,
what exactly do you mean by that?

00:05:47.674 --> 00:05:52.128
He likely would have said something
about how I should be more confident, or

00:05:52.128 --> 00:05:55.345
how I should be more aggressive
with my good opinions.

00:05:55.345 --> 00:05:59.392
No malintent, great.

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:04.942
And third,
if you have the emotional bandwidth to,

00:06:04.942 --> 00:06:08.866
state how the comment impacted you.

00:06:08.866 --> 00:06:14.395
In one of the GSB's flagship classes on
interpersonal dynamics, we always talk

00:06:14.395 --> 00:06:19.861
about how I cannot assume your intent,
since that's something only you know.

00:06:19.861 --> 00:06:24.019
And that you cannot assume the impact
that your words have on me,

00:06:24.019 --> 00:06:26.493
since that's something only I know.

00:06:26.493 --> 00:06:32.639
In the class, we call these
assumptions crossing the net.

00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:37.645
In the case of a microaggression,
if it is productive to state how

00:06:37.645 --> 00:06:42.560
the comment made you feel,
it can help the perpetrator learn and

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:46.121
perhaps change their behavior for
next time.

00:06:46.121 --> 00:06:50.754
In my example,
I could have simply told my boss,

00:06:50.754 --> 00:06:57.083
when you tell me to act like a man,
I feel frustrated and dejected.

00:06:57.083 --> 00:07:01.640
Because I can never be
enough due to who I am.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.924
I did eventually confront my boss and
tell him this, and

00:07:05.924 --> 00:07:08.303
I remember he looked shocked.

00:07:08.303 --> 00:07:13.472
He fumbled and admitted that he hadn't
realized that's how I had taken it,

00:07:13.472 --> 00:07:16.394
and that his intent was
just to be helpful.

00:07:16.394 --> 00:07:20.530
He did apologize for
the effect that his advice had on me, and

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:23.869
I immediately felt like
a part of the team again.

00:07:24.884 --> 00:07:28.910
Let's quickly turn to the giving end.

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:33.644
A couple years ago I was joking with my
friends in Seattle about our experiences

00:07:33.644 --> 00:07:38.466
living there, when my friend made a
comment about the lack of diversity there.

00:07:38.466 --> 00:07:41.081
To which I said, [LAUGH] what do you care?

00:07:41.081 --> 00:07:42.814
You're just a straight, white man.

00:07:45.708 --> 00:07:48.022
I watched his face fall, and

00:07:48.022 --> 00:07:53.430
immediately knew that I had crossed
a line that I hadn't meant to.

00:07:55.254 --> 00:08:00.188
Being the perpetrator of more than a few
of these kinds of careless comments

00:08:00.188 --> 00:08:04.885
has taught me three things that you
can do when you find that you've just

00:08:04.885 --> 00:08:07.016
committed a microaggression.

00:08:07.016 --> 00:08:12.807
First, accept the other person's truth.

00:08:12.807 --> 00:08:19.187
If they tell you how you made them feel,
don't cross the net.

00:08:19.187 --> 00:08:24.493
Trust their reality and use it to
inform how you show up next time.

00:08:24.493 --> 00:08:27.568
Which leads me to number two,

00:08:27.568 --> 00:08:32.612
be open to change and
learn from the experience.

00:08:32.612 --> 00:08:36.025
Not only can that help you
do better next time, but

00:08:36.025 --> 00:08:39.764
that can help you become
a better ally if you see similar

00:08:39.764 --> 00:08:43.027
microaggressions being
committed by others.

00:08:43.027 --> 00:08:48.710
And third,
if you find yourself feeling guilty,

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:51.969
remember we are only human.

00:08:51.969 --> 00:08:54.876
Everybody makes mistakes.

00:08:54.876 --> 00:08:57.699
Microaggressions are largely
unintentional.

00:08:57.699 --> 00:09:03.962
And their impact is not even uniform
across members of the same group.

00:09:03.962 --> 00:09:11.004
Different comments land with
different people in different ways.

00:09:11.004 --> 00:09:16.242
And while it's more than okay to be
curious about where I'm from, the decision

00:09:16.242 --> 00:09:21.649
to reject my truth turns ignorance into
malice, and small pinches into a bruise.

00:09:25.947 --> 00:09:29.473
It would be naive to think that we
can completely rid the world of

00:09:29.473 --> 00:09:30.680
microaggressions.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:34.601
But we can react to them
in a more productive way,

00:09:34.601 --> 00:09:41.309
by strengthening our muscles of asking
the questions and trusting the responses.

00:09:41.309 --> 00:09:46.384
It is my hope that we are all
better equipped to handle this

00:09:46.384 --> 00:09:53.278
world's inevitable imperfect situations
with greater empathy and trust.

00:09:53.278 --> 00:09:57.835
So that nobody feels like they

00:09:57.835 --> 00:10:02.216
don't belong, thank you.

00:10:02.216 --> 00:10:03.159
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
[MUSIC]

